at that's how  that decade went now if we go back to  the 40s it was a different environment  because  most of the inflation was driven by  fiscal spending whereas in the 70s it  was a combination of a lot of things as  part of the fiscal spending a lot of it  was bank loan growth right whereas in  the 40s it was almost entirely gigantic  war spending  and so we often think of war spending as  foreign spending but actually most of it  is spending on the domestic economy  they're building factories they're  paying for gi bills uh you know a lot of  the programs are somewhat similar to  what we saw over the past year and a  half  but obviously tailored towards more  industrial purposes  and so you had this this massive  increase the money supply from the  fiscal spending which was monetized by  the fed and so you had an actual you had  an inflationary component especially  because also every country in the world  was trying to get as many commodities as  they could right so there's a lot of  commodity demand  and so you had a very inflation  environment the difference was that  because federal debt as a percentage of  gdp was a hundred and thirty percent  they could raise interest rates to five  percent or ten percent or higher because  it would bankrupt the government so they  they had no choice they just had the  whole interest rates low  despite the fact that inflation was high  and so if you were holding cash or  treasuries you just got annihilated on  an inflation-adjusted basis for like  that for like a nine year period where  they just held rates super low even  though inflation peaked at 19  year-over-year in the 40s uh and the  overall average inflation for that  decade was about six percent and you  just sat there earning roughly zero  percent of your t bills and roughly two  and a half percent on your longer longer  dated treasury bonds so when you look at  this uh you had a second tweet that  basically said this is the widest gap  that we've had on record 